<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">al-Mu'azzam Isa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1176">1176</a>–<a href="/years/1227">1227</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">al-Mu'azzam Isa was the Ayyubid ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> from 1218 to 1227, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-adil">al-Adil</a> and nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-crusade">Fifth Crusade</a> he had the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> dismantled in 1219 so that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> could not hold it. He quarrelled with his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kamil">al-Kamil</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and was a noted scholar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> law.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-muazzam-isa.jpg" alt="al-Mu'azzam Isa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yaakov Shoham · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dome_temple-mount_ws.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sharaf ad-Din al-Mu'azzam Isa was installed by his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> al-Adil I, as governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> in 1198 or 1200, and by 1204 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> was his main residence. In 1217-1218 he supported his father against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fifth-crusade">Fifth Crusade</a>: he occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a> to screen Jerusalem, destroyed the fortifications of Tabor to free its garrison, and won a minor battle near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramla">Ramla</a> before returning to Damascus on news of his father's death. He was respected as a man of letters with an interest in grammar and jurisprudence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Acknowledging the suzerainty of his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kamil">al-Kamil</a>, the new sultan, he took his army to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in February 1219 to help defend it against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> at Damietta. In 1220 he captured and destroyed Caesarea, and he was in Egypt again when the Crusaders surrendered Damietta in September 1221. With the threat lifted, quarrels within the dynasty resumed. He besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hama">Hama</a> in January 1223 and took Maarat al-Numan and Salamya, but the sultan made him give them back.</p><p>He then allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muzaffar-al-din-gokbori">Gökböri</a>, ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erbil">Erbil</a>, against his brother al-Ashraf in the Jazira. In 1226 he attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> while Gökböri assaulted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>, and al-Ashraf submitted, but in May 1227 he escaped from captivity in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. With the other Ayyubid princes ranged against him and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> approaching, al-Mu'azzam died on 12 November 1227, mourned by his soldiers and the people of Damascus. His son an-Nasir Dawud succeeded him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>'s Haram ash-Sharif he extended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dome-of-the-rock">Dome of the Rock</a> terrace by 18 metres westward, restored the portico of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-aqsa-mosque">al-Aqsa Mosque</a> with a dome over its main entrance, and gave a cistern and a sebil, a public fountain. He founded the Grammarians' Madrasa in 1207, the al-Muʿaẓẓamīya Madrasa for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanafi-school">Hanafi</a> law, now the al-Mujahidin Mosque, and a madrasa over the Golden Gate named after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> his family mausoleum stands in the al-Muʿaẓẓamīya Madrasa at as-Salihiyyah, and in 1226 he rebuilt the city's wall.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Mu'azzam Isa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Al-Mu'azzam Isa</b> (1218-1227), a Hanafi jurist, built schools and dismantled the walls of Jerusalem in 1219 to keep them from the Crusaders.</blockquote><a href="/state/ayyubid-emirate-of-damascus">Ayyubid Emirate of Damascus</a></li><li><blockquote>Al-Nasir Dawud, son of <b>al-Mu'azzam Isa</b> of Damascus, lost Damascus to his uncles in 1229 but kept Kerak and the lands east of the Dead Sea, which became a separate principality.</blockquote><a href="/state/ayyubid-emirate-of-kerak">Ayyubid Emirate of Kerak</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cairo, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1209" aria-label="Cairo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of al-Mu'azzam Isa"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5091 4996h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5091 4996h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.04,31.24&amp;year=1209">What was here in 1209? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
